# C++ Linked Lists Cheatsheet

```cpp
struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode* next;
    ListNode() : val(0), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
    ListNode(int x, ListNode* next) : val(x), next(next) {}
};
```

## Pointer access
```cpp
node->val           // member via pointer (sugar for (*node).val)
node->next          // next node
ListNode obj(0);    // OBJECT on stack (is a node)
ListNode* p = &obj; // POINTER to that object (same memory)
ListNode* p(0);     // GOTCHA: pointer set to nullptr, NOT a new node
ListNode* p = new ListNode(0);  // pointer to new heap node
```

## Build-as-you-go pattern (always two lines together)
```cpp
trav->next = node;   // connect
trav = trav->next;   // ADVANCE (forgetting this overwrites on next step)
```

## Dummy node (unifies "head" with "middle" cases)
```cpp
ListNode dummy(0, head);   // stack dummy before head, auto-destroyed
ListNode* trav = &dummy;
// ... build/modify ...
return dummy.next;         // correct head whether or not head changed
```
Use for: insert/delete that might hit the head, merging, building new lists.

## Reverse list (prev / curr / next)
```cpp
ListNode* prev = nullptr;
ListNode* curr = head;
while (curr != nullptr) {
    ListNode* nxt = curr->next;   // capture BEFORE breaking link
    curr->next = prev;            // flip pointer
    prev = curr;                  // advance prev
    curr = nxt;                   // advance curr
}
return prev;
// Time O(n), Space O(1)
```

## Merge two sorted lists
```cpp
ListNode dummy(0);
ListNode* trav = &dummy;
while (l1 && l2) {
    if (l1->val < l2->val) { trav->next = l1; l1 = l1->next; }
    else                   { trav->next = l2; l2 = l2->next; }
    trav = trav->next;
}
trav->next = (l1 != nullptr) ? l1 : l2;  // remaining nodes already linked
return dummy.next;
// Time O(n+m), Space O(1)
```

## Delete node given only that node (not the last)
```cpp
ListNode* toDelete = node->next;
node->val = toDelete->val;     // copy next's data into current
node->next = toDelete->next;   // bypass next
delete toDelete;               // free it
```

## Two pointers: fast / slow

### Remove Nth from end (one pass)
```cpp
ListNode dummy(0, head);
ListNode* slow = &dummy;
ListNode* fast = &dummy;
for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) fast = fast->next;  // gap of n+1
while (fast != nullptr) { slow = slow->next; fast = fast->next; }
ListNode* toDelete = slow->next;
slow->next = toDelete->next;
delete toDelete;
return dummy.next;
// slow ends at node BEFORE the one to remove
```

### Middle of list / cycle detection
```cpp
// middle: slow lands at middle when fast hits end
ListNode* slow = head;
ListNode* fast = head;
while (fast && fast->next) {
    slow = slow->next;        // 1x
    fast = fast->next->next;  // 2x
}
// slow is at middle (cycle: if slow == fast at any point, there's a loop)
```

## Common bugs (today's lessons)
- `ListNode* p(0)` makes a NULL pointer, not a node. Use `new ListNode(0)` or stack object.
- Forgetting `trav = trav->next` when building: each assign overwrites the last, nodes get lost.
- In reverse, capture `next` BEFORE flipping the pointer or you lose the rest of the list.
- Off-by-one when stopping to delete: you must land on the node BEFORE the target (singly linked has no prev). Dummy node + correct gap fixes this.

## When dummy node helps
Any operation that could affect the head: delete head, insert before head,
build a fresh list. The dummy gives you a stable "node before head" so the
head case needs no special handling.

It's basically to treat the head as part of the body and avoid writing that special case

## Stack vs heap for dummy
```cpp
ListNode dummy(0);        // stack: auto-cleanup, preferred for temp dummy
ListNode* d = new ...;    // heap: needs delete, only if it must outlive scope
```
